INT. REGENCY MUSIC ROOM, HARTLEIGH PARK - AFTERNOON

A long room with a gleaming pianoforte, gilt chairs, and French windows open to a garden of clipped yews. Sunlight lays long stripes across the parquet. A clock chimes four. Lavender and beeswax.

Six people form a tableau: LADY ARABELLA FOTHERINGHAY-ADEYEMI, thirty, in primrose muslin, seated at the instrument. Her mother, DOWAGER LADY HESTER FOTHERINGHAY-OKORO, sixty, rigid in a high-backed chair with a lorgnette. The curate MR. ELIAS THISTLEWOOD-BELL, twenty-five, turning pages. The cousin MISS PHILOMENA CARRADINE-LUU, nineteen, embroidering by the window. The butler MR. OSWIN PRATT-NWOSU, fifty, carrying a tray of tea. And a gentleman caller, standing in the doorway with his hat in his hands.

Arabella plays a gentle air. The Dowager nods in time. Elias turns a page. Philomena stitches. Oswin sets down the tray.

The caller clears his throat. Everyone turns.

CALLER
Forgive the intrusion. I come with a letter for Lady Arabella.

DOWAGER
From whom, sir?

CALLER
From a man who was thought drowned.

Arabella's hands stop. The instrument falls silent. The clock ticks. Oswin's cup rattles in its saucer.

ARABELLA
His name, sir.

CALLER
(smiling)
Captain Edmund Harcourt-Reyes.

He bows. The Dowager's lorgnette clatters to the floor.

DOWAGER
Edmund?

HARCOURT
Alive, madam. And entirely at your service.

Arabella rises. The garden breeze lifts the curtains. For a moment no one in the room breathes.
